\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Graded spin carriers in even and odd lattice dimensions}
\date{2026-11-09}

\p{If #{d=2\ell}, write #{P=\gamma_1\cdots\gamma_d}. As in
\ref{fdip-009X}, #{P^{\dagger}=(-1)^\ell P} and
#{P^2=(-1)^\ell\bbI}. The phase #{\Gamma=\ui^\ell P}, written #{C}
in \ref{fdip-009X}, is the grading of \ref{fdip-00IN}:
#{\Gamma^{\dagger}=\Gamma}, #{\Gamma^2=\bbI}, and
#{\Gamma\gamma_j\Gamma=-\gamma_j}. Each plane bivector
#{B_r=\gamma_{2r-1}\gamma_{2r}} squares to #{-\bbI}: the two
generators anticommute and each squares to #{\bbI}. Since the
generators are self-adjoint, #{B_r^{\dagger}=-B_r}. The
Hermitian comparison on that plane is #{H_r=\ui B_r}, so
#{H_r^2=\bbI} and #{H_r^{\dagger}=H_r}. These commuting
involutions have an orthogonal joint eigenspace decomposition
indexed by #{\{\pm1\}^\ell}. The invertible operator
#{\gamma_{2r-1}} reverses the #{r}th eigenvalue and preserves
all the others. Thus all joint eigenspaces have the same
dimension. One is nonzero, so every nonzero carrier has
dimension divisible by #{2^\ell}.}

\p{In the standard basis of #{\mathbb C^2}, take
##{X=\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\1&0\end{pmatrix},\quad
Y=\begin{pmatrix}0&-\ui\\\ui&0\end{pmatrix},\quad
Z=\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\0&-1\end{pmatrix}.}
On #{(\mathbb C^2)^{\otimes\ell}} set
##{\begin{aligned}
\gamma_{2r-1}&=Z^{\otimes(r-1)}\otimes X\otimes \bbI^{\otimes(\ell-r)},\\
\gamma_{2r}&=Z^{\otimes(r-1)}\otimes Y\otimes \bbI^{\otimes(\ell-r)}.
\end{aligned}}
Their squares and adjoints are #{\bbI} and themselves. For distinct generators, the first tensor factor where their nonidentity patterns differ contributes exactly one anticommutation. These operators therefore realize dimension #{2^\ell}, proving minimality. The tensor carrier in [Covariant centered differences with an internal fiber](fdip-009O) instead has #{n_s=2^d}; in dimension #{4} it has 16 components, rather than the 4 components of one minimal Dirac module. The chosen multiplicity remains part of the field data.}

\p{For odd #{d}, begin with any nonzero Hermitian Clifford module #{W} with endomorphisms #{\gamma'_j}. Such a module is explicit: for #{d=2\ell+1}, add the even-dimensional chirality operator to the preceding #{2\ell} generators; when #{\ell=0}, use #{W=\mathbb C} and #{\gamma'_1=1}. On #{S=W\oplus W} set
##{\gamma_j=\begin{pmatrix}\gamma'_j&0\\0&-\gamma'_j\end{pmatrix},\qquad
\Gamma=\begin{pmatrix}0&\bbI_W\\\bbI_W&0\end{pmatrix}.}}

\p{Block multiplication verifies all the displayed Clifford and grading relations, and #{n_s=2\dim W}. Thus a supplied graded module is available in odd lattice dimensions as well. More generally, anticommutation with the invertible #{\gamma_1} exchanges the two eigenspaces of any supplied #{\Gamma}, so their dimensions agree. None of the determinant, source or Ward arguments requires even #{d}.}
